SUID Privilege Escalation via Environment Variable Injection

Author: Tomash Popov

Target: Local Privilege Escalation (aphrodite -> ariadne) Hackmyvm.eu

Technique: Command Injection, Environment Variable Manipulation, SUID Exploitation
📌 Challenge Overview

The objective of this challenge is to escalate privileges from the initial user (aphrodite) to the target user (ariadne) on the hades machine. The critical clue is a custom SUID binary named homecontent, which we must exploit without the aid of standard debugging tools like strings or ltrace.
🔍 Enumeration & Black-Box Analysis

Upon accessing the system as aphrodite, I checked the contents of the current directory and reviewed the objective in mission.txt:

    "The user ariadne knows what we keep in our HOME."

The directory contained the following files:

    ariadne_pass.txt (Target file, likely lacking read permissions for aphrodite)

    flagz.txt

    homecontent (SUID binary)

    mission.txt

Running the binary natively produced the following output:
Bash

aphrodite@hades:/pwned/aphrodite$ ./homecontent
The content of your HOME is:
ariadne_pass.txt  flagz.txt  homecontent  mission.txt

The Constraint

A significant hurdle in this challenge was the restricted environment. Standard binary analysis tools (ltrace, strace, strings) were unavailable or restricted. I had to treat the binary as a black box and deduce its internal mechanics based on inputs and outputs.
Hypothesis

Observing the output, the binary prints "The content of your HOME is:" followed by a directory listing. It strongly suggests the binary is executing a system command akin to:
Bash

echo "The content of your HOME is:"
ls $HOME

Because the binary has the SUID bit set, it executes this underlying command with the privileges of its owner (presumably ariadne or root). If the binary does not sanitize the $HOME environment variable before passing it to the system shell, it is vulnerable to command injection.
🎯 Exploitation

To test this hypothesis, I attempted to manipulate the $HOME environment variable. By injecting a command separator (;), I could force the binary to execute a secondary command of my choosing under its elevated context.

Instead of passing a valid directory, I crafted the HOME variable to terminate the expected ls command and append a cat command aimed at the target password file.

Payload Construction:
Bash

export HOME="; cat ariadne_pass.txt"

Execution:
Bash

aphrodite@hades:/pwned/aphrodite$ ./homecontent
The content of your HOME is:
ariadne_pass.txt  flagz.txt  homecontent  mission.txt
iNgNazuJrmhJKWixktzk

💡 Conclusion

The vulnerability stems from the SUID binary insecurely calling system commands using user-controlled environment variables ($HOME). By injecting the ; character, the shell interpreted the variable as two separate instructions: ls "" and cat ariadne_pass.txt.

Because the binary was executing with SUID privileges, the cat command successfully bypassed the file read restrictions, dumping the password iNgNazuJrmhJKWixktzk and allowing lateral movement to the ariadne user.
